{
  "notes": "<h3>Algebra of limits</h3><ul><li>$\\lim[f(x)\\pm g(x)]=\\lim f(x)\\pm\\lim g(x)$; $\\lim[f(x)g(x)]=\\lim f(x)\\cdot\\lim g(x)$; $\\lim\\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}=\\dfrac{\\lim f(x)}{\\lim g(x)}$ provided $\\lim g(x)\\neq0$.</li></ul><h3>Standard limits</h3><table><tr><th>Limit</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to a}\\dfrac{x^n-a^n}{x-a}$</td><td>$na^{n-1}$</td></tr><tr><td>$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to0}\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x}$</td><td>$1$</td></tr><tr><td>$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to0}\\dfrac{\\tan x}{x}$</td><td>$1$</td></tr><tr><td>$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to0}\\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{x}$</td><td>$0$</td></tr><tr><td>$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to0}\\dfrac{e^x-1}{x}$</td><td>$1$</td></tr><tr><td>$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to0}\\dfrac{\\log(1+x)}{x}$</td><td>$1$</td></tr></table><ul><li>For limits at $x\\to\\infty$ of a ratio of polynomials, divide by the highest power of $x$; the limit is the ratio of leading coefficients if degrees are equal, 0 if numerator's degree is smaller, and $\\pm\\infty$ if larger.</li><li>Trick: for $\\sin(kx)/x$ type forms, write as $k\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sin(kx)}{kx}\\to k$; similarly for $\\tan$, $e^{kx}-1$, $\\log(1+kx)$.</li></ul><h3>Derivative from first principles</h3><ul><li>$f'(x)=\\lim\\limits_{h\\to0}\\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}$.</li></ul><h3>Standard derivatives</h3><ul><li>$\\dfrac{d}{dx}x^n=nx^{n-1}$; $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\sin x=\\cos x$; $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\cos x=-\\sin x$; $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\tan x=\\sec^2x$; $\\dfrac{d}{dx}e^x=e^x$; $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\log x=\\dfrac1x$; derivative of a constant is 0.</li></ul><h3>Product and quotient rules</h3><ul><li>$(uv)' = u'v+uv'$.</li><li>$\\left(\\dfrac{u}{v}\\right)' = \\dfrac{u'v-uv'}{v^2}$.</li></ul><h3>Exam tip</h3><p>KCET typically asks 2-3 questions from this chapter, split between evaluating a standard limit (trig, exponential/log, or algebraic) and finding a derivative by first principles or using product/quotient rule. Simplify the function first (e.g. split a fraction) before differentiating — it is often faster than the full quotient rule.</p>",
